      "side": 1,
      "message": "I wanted to, but I couldn\u0027t find any existing example in our code where `SK_GUARDED_BY` points at a function instead of an object. Do we have any team experts for the thread annotations? I looked into this earlier and came up pretty empty-handed.\n\n`SkMutex` used to be trivial, so I could have made a static-global `SkMutex` instead of making a function like this, but at http://review.skia.org/507998 I gave it a destructor and made it non-trivial. :( \n\n(As a point of reference, I checked what `absl::Mutex` does--it is also non-trivial and has ctors/dtors that do real work: http://google3/third_party/absl/synchronization/mutex.cc;l\u003d773;rcl\u003d465287204 )\n\nI could probably set up a class which wraps the singleton objects, and then use the thread annotations within the class? It\u0027d be structured a little differently but it\u0027s worth extra complexity if we can get the thread tracking.",
